ze\n        self._queue = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[_T]\n        self._pending_tasks = 0\n        self._finished = Event()\n        self._finished.set()\n        self._putters = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[_ReleasingContextManager]\n        self._getters = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[_ReleasingContextManager]\n        self._unfinished_tasks = 0\n\n    ...\n    async def get(self) -> _T:\n        \"\"\"Remove and return an item from the queue.\n\n        If queue is empty, wait until an item is available.\n\n        .. versionadded:: 6.0\n        \"\"\"\n        while self.empty():\n            getter = _ReleasingContextManager(self._getters)\n            try:\n                await getter\n            except exceptions.CancelledError:\n                raise\n        return self.get_nowait()\n```\n\nHmm no, that's not right either. I keep flip-flopping. Let me just use the get_info tool \u2014 that's what it's for. I'll query:\n- `Queue` (class info)\n- `Queue._format`\n- `Queue.get`\n- `Queue.put`\n- `Queue.put_nowait`\n- `Queue.task_done`\n- `Queue.__repr__`\n- `_ReleasingContextManager` maybe\n\nLet me do that first, then write the tests based on actual signatures.\n\nRegarding the event loop: tornado 6 requires a running IOLoop/asyncio loop for `await`. Using `asyncio.run` should be fine. But there's a subtlety: tornado's `Queue.get` in 6.x uses `await` on tornado Futures which are asyncio Futures; when run under `asyncio.run`, the default event loop policy is asyncio's, and tornado 6 works fine with plain asyncio loops (IOLoop wraps asyncio loop). Yes, tornado 6 is fully asyncio-based.\n\nAlternatively, to be extra safe, use `tornado.platform.asyncio.AsyncIOMainLoop`? Not needed; tornado 6's IOLoop is asyncio-based by default.\n\nActually, wait. There's another consideration: `tornado.queues.Queue.get` when awaited creates a future appended to `_getters` and awaits it. When we `await asyncio.sleep(0)`, the get coroutine runs until it awaits the future, so `_getters` gets populated. Good.\n\nFor putters: `q.put(item)` on a full queue appends to `_putters` and awaits. After `await asyncio.sleep(0)`, `_putters` has one entry. Good.\n\nBut what exactly is stored in `_putters`? If it's `(item, future)` tuples, then `len(self._putters)` is 1 and repr shows \"putters[1]\". If it's context managers or futures, same length. Either way, the repr check \"putters[1]\" works. Good \u2014 I don't need to know the internal type for the assertion, just for cleanup. Cleanup: cancel the tasks and await them; the `put` coroutine's cancellation handling removes itself from `_putters`? In tornado's implementation, on CancelledError the putter removes itself... Actually in 6.x:\n\n```python\n    async def put(self, item, timeout=None):\n        while self.full():\n            putter = _ReleasingContextManager(self._putters)\n            try:\n                await putter\n            except exceptions.CancelledError:\n                ...\n```\n\nHmm. Whatever \u2014 cancelling the task will propagate; the deque entry might remain but the queue is local to the test, so no state pollution. The important cleanup is: don't leave pending tasks on the global loop, and don't leave warnings. Since each test creates its own queue and its own loop (via asyncio.run), state pollution is minimal. But `asyncio.run` creates and closes a new loop each time \u2014 fine.\n\nOne concern: `asyncio.run` with tornado \u2014 tornado's `Event` (used in Queue for `_finished`) uses `asyncio.Event`? In tornado 6, `tornado.queues.Event` is tornado's own class wrapping futures. It works under asyncio loop. Tornado's own tests run queues under tornado's gen_test which uses IOLoop (asyncio-based). asyncio.run should be equivalent.\n\nHmm, actually wait: there might be an issue with `asyncio.run` and tornado's `Future` if tornado is in \"wsgi\" or twisted mode \u2014 no, default is asyncio.\n\nLet me also double check: does `repr(q)` call `_format`? In tornado:\n\n```python\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return \"<%s %s at %s>\" % (self.__class__.__name__, self._format(), hex(id(self)))\n```\n\nYes. So `repr(q)` covers `_format`.\n\nNow, about covering the *branches*: The missing lines 337-346 include the conditions. For branch coverage:\n- 338: true (queue non-empty) and false (empty queue).\n- 340: true (getters) and false (no getters).\n- 342: true (putters) and false (no putters).\n- 344: true (unfinished tasks) and false (no tasks).\n\nI'll write tests:\n\n1. `test_repr_empty_queue`: `Queue(maxsize=5)`, repr \u2192 contains \"maxsize=5\", not \"queue=\", not \"getters\", not \"putters\", not \"tasks=\". Covers false branches.\n\n2. `test_repr_queue_with_items_and_tasks`: `q = Queue()`, `q.put_nowait(\"a\")`, `q.put_nowait(\"b\")` \u2192 repr contains \"queue=deque(['a', 'b'])\" and \"tasks=2\". Then `q.task_done(); q.task_done()` \u2192 repr no longer contains \"tasks=\". Also covers task_done cleanup. Hmm, but `task_done` when `_unfinished_tasks` reaches 0 sets `_finished`. Fine.\n\nWait \u2014 does `put_nowait` increment `_unfinished_tasks`? Let me verify with get_info. In tornado:\n\n```python\n    def put_nowait(self, item: _T) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Put an item into the queue without blocking.\n\n        If no free slot is immediately available, raise `QueueFull`.\n\n        Otherwise, put the item in the queue and return immediately.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._put(item)\n        self._unfinished_tasks += 1\n        self._finished.clear()\n        self._wakeup_next(self._getters)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall in tornado 6:\n\n```python\n    def put_nowait(self, item: _T) -> None:\n        self._put(item)\n        self._unfinished_tasks += 1\n        self._finished.clear()\n        self._wakeup_next(self._getters)\n```\n\nYes, I'm fairly confident. And `task_done`:\n\n```python\n    def task_done(self) -> None:\n        if self._unfinished_tasks <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"task_done() called too many times\")\n        self._unfinished_tasks -= 1\n        if self._unfinished_tasks == 0:\n            self._finished.set()\n```\n\nOK. I'll verify with get_info.\n\n3. `test_repr_with_getters`: async \u2014 start `q.get()` on empty queue, sleep(0), repr contains \"getters[1]\". Cleanup: cancel.\n\n4. `test_repr_with_putters`: async \u2014 full queue, start `q.put(item)`, sleep(0), repr contains \"putters[1]\". Cleanup: cancel.\n\n5. Maybe a combined test covering everything at once: queue with item + putter + unfinished tasks, and a second queue with getter. Actually, can I have queue non-empty AND putters non-empty on the same queue? Yes: maxsize=1, put_nowait(\"x\") (queue non-empty, tasks=1), then put(\"y\") waits (putters=1). So one queue covers 338-339, 342-343, 344-345. And getters on the same queue? Queue is full and non-empty, so `get()` returns immediately \u2014 can't have a getter on the same queue via public API. Unless... `get()` on a non-empty queue returns immediately, so no getter. So getters need a separate empty queue, or direct manipulation.\n\nHmm, alternatively: start a getter on an empty queue, then directly `q._queue.append(item)` without waking getters. Then repr shows queue + getters. But that's internal manipulation. It's fine for a coverage test, but I'd rather keep it clean with two queues in one test, or separate tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe do one comprehensive async test:\n\n```python\ndef test_repr_full_format():\n    async def main():\n        # Queue with item, putter waiting, unfinished tasks\n        q = Queue(maxsize=1)\n        q.put_nowait(\"a\")\n        putter = asyncio.ensure_future(q.put(\"b\"))\n        # Separate queue with a getter waiting\n        q2 = Queue()\n        getter = asyncio.ensure_future(q2.get())\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n        r = repr(q)\n        assert \"maxsize=1\" in r\n        assert \"queue=deque(['a'])\" in r\n        assert \"putters[1]\" in r\n        assert \"tasks=1\" in r\n        assert \"getters\" not in r\n        r2 = repr(q2)\n        assert \"getters[1]\" in r2\n        assert \"queue=\" not in r2\n        # cleanup\n        putter.cancel()\n        getter.cancel()\n        await asyncio.gather(putter, getter, return_exceptions=True)\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety with cancelling `q.put(\"b\")`: when cancelled, does the entry stay in `_putters`? If it stays, no problem since q is local. But if the cancellation handling in tornado pops it... doesn't matter.\n\nBut wait, there's another subtlety: after cancelling, `asyncio.gather(..., return_exceptions=True)` awaits the cancelled tasks. The `put` coroutine, upon cancellation, might raise CancelledError \u2014 gathered with return_exceptions=True, fine.\n\nAlso, in tornado 6.3+, `put` might check `self._closed` \u2014 not relevant.\n\nAnother subtlety: `asyncio.ensure_future(q.put(\"b\"))` \u2014 `q.put` is a coroutine; ensure_future schedules it. After `await asyncio.sleep(0.01)`, it has run to the point of awaiting. Good.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 does `q.put(\"b\")` on a full queue actually wait, or does it raise? `put` waits when full (maxsize=1, one item \u2192 full). Yes.\n\nNow, is there an issue with using `asyncio.run` when tornado's IOLoop hasn't been configured? Tornado 6's Queue uses `tornado.concurrent.Future` = `asyncio.Future`, and `Event` from tornado.queues:\n\n```python\nclass Event:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._future = Future()\n        self._waiters = ...\n```\n\nHmm, tornado.queues.Event:\n\n```python\nclass Event:\n    \"\"\"An event blocks coroutines until their internal flag is set to True.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._value = False\n        self._waiters = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[Future[None]]\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return \"<%s %s>\" % (self.__class__.__name__, \"set\" if self._value else \"clear\")\n\n    def is_set(self) -> bool:\n        return self._value\n\n    def set(self) -> None:\n        self._value = True\n        for f in self._waiters:\n            if not f.done():\n                f.set_result(None)\n\n    def clear(self) -> None:\n        self._value = False\n\n    async def wait(self) -> None:\n        if self._value:\n            return\n        f = Future()\n        self._waiters.append(f)\n        await f\n```\n\nAll asyncio-compatible. Good.\n\nNow, `Queue.__init__` creates `Event()` and sets it. `put_nowait` calls `self._finished.clear()`. No loop interaction. Good.\n\nSo `asyncio.run(main())` works for tornado 6.x. But what if the environment has tornado 5.x? In tornado 5, Futures were tornado-specific and needed IOLoop. But the line numbers (337-346) suggest tornado 6.3+. In tornado 5, queues.py was much shorter (~300 lines total). So tornado 6.x it is. I'll use `asyncio.run`.\n\nHmm, but actually, to be safest, maybe use `tornado.ioloop.IOLoop.run_sync`? That's deprecated in 6.2 but still works. Or `IOLoop.current().run_sync(main)`. In tornado 6.2+, calling `IOLoop.current()` outside a running loop issues a DeprecationWarning but works. `asyncio.run` is cleaner and recommended. Tornado docs say: \"asyncio.run(main())\" is fine for tornado 6.\n\nAlternatively, use pytest-asyncio if available... but I can't be sure it's installed. The safest bet that works with plain pytest: sync test functions calling `asyncio.run`. Let me go with that.\n\nWait, one more consideration: `asyncio.run` requires no running loop in the thread. Under pytest, no loop is running by default. Good.\n\nAlso, `asyncio.ensure_future` inside `main()` uses the running loop. Good.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `repr` of the queue's `_queue` deque: `queue=deque(['a'])`. The format is `\" queue=%r\" % self._queue` \u2192 `deque(['a'])`. So assertion: `\"queue=deque(['a'])\" in r`. Good.\n\nFor maxsize: `\"maxsize=%r\" % (self.maxsize,)` \u2192 `maxsize=1`. `self.maxsize` is a property returning `se