rnado:\n\n```python\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return \"<%s %s at %s>\" % (self.__class__.__name__, self._format(), hex(id(self)))\n```\n\nYes. So `repr(q)` covers `_format`.\n\nNow, about covering the *branches*: The missing lines 337-346 include the conditions. For branch coverage:\n- 338: true (queue non-empty) and false (empty queue).\n- 340: true (getters) and false (no getters).\n- 342: true (putters) and false (no putters).\n- 344: true (unfinished tasks) and false (no tasks).\n\nI'll write tests:\n\n1. `test_repr_empty_queue`: `Queue(maxsize=5)`, repr \u2192 contains \"maxsize=5\", not \"queue=\", not \"getters\", not \"putters\", not \"tasks=\". Covers false branches.\n\n2. `test_repr_queue_with_items_and_tasks`: `q = Queue()`, `q.put_nowait(\"a\")`, `q.put_nowait(\"b\")` \u2192 repr contains \"queue=deque(['a', 'b'])\" and \"tasks=2\". Then `q.task_done(); q.task_done()` \u2192 repr no longer contains \"tasks=\". Also covers task_done cleanup. Hmm, but `task_done` when `_unfinished_tasks` reaches 0 sets `_finished`. Fine.\n\nWait \u2014 does `put_nowait` increment `_unfinished_tasks`? Let me verify with get_info. In tornado:\n\n```python\n    def put_nowait(self, item: _T) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Put an item into the queue without blocking.\n\n        If no free slot is immediately available, raise `QueueFull`.\n\n        Otherwise, put the item in the queue and return immediately.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._put(item)\n        self._unfinished_tasks += 1\n        self._finished.clear()\n        self._wakeup_next(self._getters)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall in tornado 6:\n\n```python\n    def put_nowait(self, item: _T) -> None:\n        self._put(item)\n        self._unfinished_tasks += 1\n        self._finished.clear()\n        self._wakeup_next(self._getters)\n```\n\nYes, I'm fairly confident. And `task_done`:\n\n```python\n    def task_done(self) -> None:\n        if self._unfinished_tasks <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"task_done() called too many times\")\n        self._unfinished_tasks -= 1\n        if self._unfinished_tasks == 0:\n            self._finished.set()\n```\n\nOK. I'll verify with get_info.\n\n3. `test_repr_with_getters`: async \u2014 start `q.get()` on empty queue, sleep(0), repr contains \"getters[1]\". Cleanup: cancel.\n\n4. `test_repr_with_putters`: async \u2014 full queue, start `q.put(item)`, sleep(0), repr contains \"putters[1]\". Cleanup: cancel.\n\n5. Maybe a combined test covering everything at once: queue with item + putter + unfinished tasks, and a second queue with getter. Actually, can I have queue non-empty AND putters non-empty on the same queue? Yes: maxsize=1, put_nowait(\"x\") (queue non-empty, tasks=1), then put(\"y\") waits (putters=1). So one queue covers 338-339, 342-343, 344-345. And getters on the same queue? Queue is full and non-empty, so `get()` returns immediately \u2014 can't have a getter on the same queue via public API. Unless... `get()` on a non-empty queue returns immediately, so no getter. So getters need a separate empty queue, or direct manipulation.\n\nHmm, alternatively: start a getter on an empty queue, then directly `q._queue.append(item)` without waking getters. Then repr shows queue + getters. But that's internal manipulation. It's fine for a coverage test, but I'd rather keep it clean with two queues in one test, or separate tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe do one comprehensive async test:\n\n```python\ndef test_repr_full_format():\n    async def main():\n        # Queue with item, putter waiting, unfinished tasks\n        q = Queue(maxsize=1)\n        q.put_nowait(\"a\")\n        putter = asyncio.ensure_future(q.put(\"b\"))\n        # Separate queue with a getter waiting\n        q2 = Queue()\n        getter = asyncio.ensure_future(q2.get())\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n        r = repr(q)\n        assert \"maxsize=1\" in r\n        assert \"queue=deque(['a'])\" in r\n        assert \"putters[1]\" in r\n        assert \"tasks=1\" in r\n        assert \"getters\" not in r\n        r2 = repr(q2)\n        assert \"getters[1]\" in r2\n        assert \"queue=\" not in r2\n        # cleanup\n        putter.cancel()\n        getter.cancel()\n        await asyncio.gather(putter, getter, return_exceptions=True)\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety with cancelling `q.put(\"b\")`: when cancelled, does the entry stay in `_putters`? If it stays, no problem since q is local. But if the cancellation handling in tornado pops it... doesn't matter.\n\nBut wait, there's another subtlety: after cancelling, `asyncio.gather(..., return_exceptions=True)` awaits the cancelled tasks. The `put` coroutine, upon cancellation, might raise CancelledError \u2014 gathered with return_exceptions=True, fine.\n\nAlso, in tornado 6.3+, `put` might check `self._closed` \u2014 not relevant.\n\nAnother subtlety: `asyncio.ensure_future(q.put(\"b\"))` \u2014 `q.put` is a coroutine; ensure_future schedules it. After `await asyncio.sleep(0.01)`, it has run to the point of awaiting. Good.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 does `q.put(\"b\")` on a full queue actually wait, or does it raise? `put` waits when full (maxsize=1, one item \u2192 full). Yes.\n\nNow, is there an issue with using `asyncio.run` when tornado's IOLoop hasn't been configured? Tornado 6's Queue uses `tornado.concurrent.Future` = `asyncio.Future`, and `Event` from tornado.queues:\n\n```python\nclass Event:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._future = Future()\n        self._waiters = ...\n```\n\nHmm, tornado.queues.Event:\n\n```python\nclass Event:\n    \"\"\"An event blocks coroutines until their internal flag is set to True.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._value = False\n        self._waiters = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[Future[None]]\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return \"<%s %s>\" % (self.__class__.__name__, \"set\" if self._value else \"clear\")\n\n    def is_set(self) -> bool:\n        return self._value\n\n    def set(self) -> None:\n        self._value = True\n        for f in self._waiters:\n            if not f.done():\n                f.set_result(None)\n\n    def clear(self) -> None:\n        self._value = False\n\n    async def wait(self) -> None:\n        if self._value:\n            return\n        f = Future()\n        self._waiters.append(f)\n        await f\n```\n\nAll asyncio-compatible. Good.\n\nNow, `Queue.__init__` creates `Event()` and sets it. `put_nowait` calls `self._finished.clear()`. No loop interaction. Good.\n\nSo `asyncio.run(main())` works for tornado 6.x. But what if the environment has tornado 5.x? In tornado 5, Futures were tornado-specific and needed IOLoop. But the line numbers (337-346) suggest tornado 6.3+. In tornado 5, queues.py was much shorter (~300 lines total). So tornado 6.x it is. I'll use `asyncio.run`.\n\nHmm, but actually, to be safest, maybe use `tornado.ioloop.IOLoop.run_sync`? That's deprecated in 6.2 but still works. Or `IOLoop.current().run_sync(main)`. In tornado 6.2+, calling `IOLoop.current()` outside a running loop issues a DeprecationWarning but works. `asyncio.run` is cleaner and recommended. Tornado docs say: \"asyncio.run(main())\" is fine for tornado 6.\n\nAlternatively, use pytest-asyncio if available... but I can't be sure it's installed. The safest bet that works with plain pytest: sync test functions calling `asyncio.run`. Let me go with that.\n\nWait, one more consideration: `asyncio.run` requires no running loop in the thread. Under pytest, no loop is running by default. Good.\n\nAlso, `asyncio.ensure_future` inside `main()` uses the running loop. Good.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `repr` of the queue's `_queue` deque: `queue=deque(['a'])`. The format is `\" queue=%r\" % self._queue` \u2192 `deque(['a'])`. So assertion: `\"queue=deque(['a'])\" in r`. Good.\n\nFor maxsize: `\"maxsize=%r\" % (self.maxsize,)` \u2192 `maxsize=1`. `self.maxsize` is a property returning `self._maxsize`. Good.\n\nNow, the full repr: `\"<Queue maxsize=1 queue=deque(['a']) putters[1] tasks=1 at 0x...>\"`. \n\nLet me now also double-check `Queue.get` behavior in 6.3 with shutdown: `get` on empty queue: `while self.empty(): if self._closed: raise QueueShutDown; ...` then appends future to `_getters` and awaits with optional timeout. Fine.\n\nAnd `put` in 6.3:\n\n```python\n    async def put(self, item: _T, timeout: Optional[float] = None) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Put an item into the queue, possibly waiting for a free slot.\n        ...\n        \"\"\"\n        future = None  # type: Optional[Future[None]]\n        timeout_handle = None\n        while self.full():\n            if self._closed:\n                raise QueueShutDown\n            if future is None:\n                future = Future()\n                self._putters.append((item, future))\n            ...\n            await future  # roughly\n        return self.put_nowait(item)\n```\n\nHmm, in 6.3 I think `_putters` holds `(item, future)` tuples so that on shutdown, pending putters can be resumed with QueueShutDown. Let me check via get_info to be sure about `_putters` structure \u2014 actually, for my test it doesn't matter; I only need `len(self._putters)` to be 1, which it will be after one waiting `put`.\n\nBut for cleanup, cancelling the put task: in 6.3, the `put` coroutine awaits the future; cancellation raises CancelledError at the await; the tuple stays in `_putters` (with a cancelled future). Since `_wakeup_next(self._putters)` skips done futures, no issue. And the queue is local. Fine.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually there's a potential issue: if the cancelled putter's tuple stays in `_putters` and later `put_nowait` is called... not in my test. Fine.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether to test `Queue` subclasses like `PriorityQueue`/`LifoQueue` \u2014 not needed; `_format` is defined on `Queue`.\n\nLet me also consider: should I test via `str(q)` or `repr(q)`? `__repr__` is what's defined; `repr(q)` calls it. Also `print(q)` uses `__str__` which defaults to `__repr__`. I'll use `repr(q)`.\n\nNow, about \"clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution\": \n- Each test creates its own Queue instances (local).\n- Async tasks are cancelled and awaited within the test.\n- `asyncio.run` closes the loop.\n- No global state modified.\n- For the tasks test, call `task_done` to reset `_unfinished_tasks` (local anyway).\n\nI think using `asyncio.run` inside sync test functions is clean. But hmm, one more thought: some CI environments run pytest with `pytest-asyncio` in auto mode, which might... no, sync functions are unaffected.\n\nAlternatively, I could write the async tests using `pytest.mark.asyncio` \u2014 but if the plugin isn't installed, the tests fail/error. `asyncio.run` approach has no dependency. Go with it.\n\nLet me now write the tests:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nfrom tornado.queues import Queue\n\n\ndef _run(coro):\n    \"\"\"Run a coroutine to completion on a fresh event loop.\"\"\"\n    return asyncio.run(coro)\n\n\ndef test_repr_empty_queue_covers_false_branches():\n    q = Queue(maxsize=7)\n    r = repr(q)\n    assert r.startswith(\"<Queue maxsize=7 at \")\n    assert \"queue=\" not in r\n    assert \"getters[\" not in r\n    assert \"putters[\" not in r\n    assert \"tasks=\" not in r\n\n\ndef test_repr_with_items_and_unfinished_tasks():\n    q = Queue()\n    q.put_nowait(\"a\")\n    q.put_nowait(\"b\")\n    r = repr(q)\n    assert \"maxsize=%r\" % q.maxsize in r\n    assert \"queue=deque(['a', 'b'])\" in r\n    assert \"tasks=2\" in r\n    # Drain and mark tasks done; repr should drop both parts.\n    assert q.get_nowait() == \"a\"\n    assert q.get_nowait() == \"b\"\n    q.task_done()\n    q.task_done()\n    r2 = repr(q)\n    assert \"queue=\" not in r2\n    assert \"tasks=\" not in r2\n\n\ndef test_repr_with_waiting_getter():\n    async def main():\n        q = Queue()\n        getter = asyncio.ensure_future(q.get())\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)  # let the getter block\n        r = repr(q)\n        assert \"getters[1]\" in r\n        assert \"queue=\" not in r\n        getter.cancel()\n        await asyncio.gather(getter, return_exceptions=True)\n        assert \"getters[\" not in repr(q)  # hmm, is this true?\n    _run(main())\n```\n\nWait \u2014 after cancelling the getter, does `_getters` become empty? In tornado 6.x, `get`:\n\n```python\n    async def get(self) -> _T:\n        while self.empty():\n            getter = _ReleasingContextManager(self._getters)\n            try:\n                await getter\n            except exceptions.CancelledError:\n                raise\n        return self.get_nowait()\n```\n\nHmm, if `_getters` is a deque of `_ReleasingContextManager` objects... wait no. Let me think about tornado 6.0's actual code. I'll look at it via get_info. Actually, let me recall tornado 6.0 queues.py more carefully. I believe it's:\n\n```python\nclass Queue(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"Coordinate producer and consumer coroutines.\n    ...\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, maxsize: Optional[int] = None) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        ...\n        \"\"\"\n        if maxsize is None:\n            maxsize = _DEFAULT_SIZE\n        self._maxsize = maxsize\n        self._queue = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[_T]\n        self._pending_tasks = 0\n        self._finished = Event()\n        self._finished.set()\n        self._putters = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[_ReleasingContextManager]\n        self._getters = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[_ReleasingContextManager]\n        self._unfinished_tasks = 0\n\n    @property\n    def maxsize(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Number of items allowed in the queue.\"\"\"\n        return self._maxsize\n\n    def qsize(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Number of items in the queue.\"\"\"\n        return len(self._queue)\n\n    def empty(self) -> bool:\n        return not self._queue\n\n    def full(self) -> bool:\n        if self.maxsize is _DEFAULT_SIZE:\n            return False\n        else:\n            return self.qsize() >= self.maxsize\n\n    async def put(self, item: _T, timeout: Optional[float] = None) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Put an item into the queue, possibly waiting for a free slot.\n        ...\n        \"\"\"\n        while self.full():\n            putter = _ReleasingContextManager(self._putters)\n            try:\n                await putter\n                if timeout is not None:  # hmm no\n                    ...\n            except exceptions.TimeoutError:\n                raise\n        return self.put_nowait(item)\n```\n\nI genuinely don't remember the exact 6.0 implementation. The `_ReleasingContextManager` thing \u2014 I think that was in the *development* version at some point. Let me check the actual tornado 6.0 release... \n\nFrom memory of tornado 6.0 queues.py (I've read this file before):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport collections\nimport heapq\nimport time\nfrom typing import Any, Awaitable, Callable, Deque, Generic, Optional, Tuple, TypeVar\n\nfrom tornado import exceptions\nfrom tornado.concurrent import Future, future_set_result_unless_cancelled\n\n_T = TypeVar(\"_T\")\n\n_NULL = object()\n\n\nclass _TimeoutGarbageCollector:\n    \"\"\"Base class for objects that use timeouts and garbage-collect them.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._waiters = collections.deque()  # type: Deque[Tuple[float, Future]]\n        self._timeouts = 0\n        self._timeout_interval = 0.1\n\n    def _garbage_collect(self) -> None:\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, that's `Condition` in locks.py. OK whatever \u2014 get_info will tell me. Let me just make the calls.\n\nActually, you know what \u2014 the exact internal representation doesn't matter much for my tests as long as I use the public API (`get`, `put`) and check `len` via the repr string. The only place internals matter is if I directly manipulate `_getters`/`_putters`, which I'm avoiding. And for the post-cancellation assertion (\"getters[\" not in repr after cancel) \u2014 that depends on whether cancellation removes the waiter from the deque. In tornado 6.x `get`:\n\nActually here's tornado 6.1's get (I'm fairly confident about this one):\n\n```python\n    async def get(self) -> _T:\n        \"\"\"Remove and return an item from the queue.\n\n        If queue is empty, wait until an item is available.\n\n        Raises `tornado.util.TimeoutError` after a timeout.\n\n        ``timeout`` may be a float representing seconds, or a `datetime.timedelta`...\n        \"\"\"\n        future = None  # type: Optional[Future[None]]\n        timeout_handle = None  # type: Optional[object]\n        while self.empty():\n            if self._closed:\n                raise QueueShutDown\n            if future is None:\n                future = Future()\n                self._getters.append(future)\n            if timeout is None:\n                await future\n            else:\n                ...\n        return self.get_nowait()\n```\n\nHmm no, that's 6.3 with shutdown. In 6.1:\n\n```python\n    async def get(self) -> _T:\n        \"\"\"Remove and return an item from the queue.\n\n        If queue is empty, wait until an item is available.\n\n        .. versionadded:: 6.0\n        \"\"\"\n        while self.empty():\n            getter = _ReleasingContextManager(self._getters)\n            try:\n                await getter\n            except exceptions.CancelledError:\n                raise\n        return self.get_nowait()\n```\n\nArgh. OK, decision: use get_info. It's provided precisely for this. Let me call:\n\n1. get_info(\"Queue\")\n2. get_info(\"Queue._format\")\n3. get_info(\"Queue.get\")\n4. get_info(\"Queue.put\")\n5. get_info(\"Queue.put_nowait\")\n6. get_info(\"Queue.task_done\")\n7. get_info(\"Queue.__repr__\")\n\nThese are all independent, so I can batch them.\n\nThen, based on results, write the final test file.\n\nLet me draft the final test file now (to be adjusted after tool results):\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Coverage tests for tornado.queues.Queue._format (lines 337-346).\"\"\"\n\nimport asyncio\n\nfrom tornado.queues import Queue\n\n\ndef _run(coro):\n    return asyncio.run(coro)\n\n\ndef test_repr_empty_queue():\n    \"\"\"False branches: no queue contents, getters, putters, or tasks.\"\"\"\n    q = Queue(maxsize=7)\n    r = repr(q)\n    assert r.startswith(\"<Queue maxsize=7 at 0x\")\n    assert \"queue=\" not in r\n    assert \"getters[\" not in r\n    assert \"putters[\" not in r\n    assert \"tasks=\" not in r\n\n\ndef test_repr_with_items_and_tasks():\n    \"\"\"True branches for _queue and _unfinished_tasks.\"\"\"\n    q = Queue()\n    q.put_nowait(\"a\")\n    q.put_nowait(\"b\")\n    r = repr(q)\n    assert \"maxsize=%r\" % (q.maxsize,) in r\n    assert \"queue=deque(['a', 'b'])\" in r\n    assert \"tasks=2\" in r\n    # After consuming items and marking tasks done, both parts disappear.\n    assert q.get_nowait() == \"a\"\n    assert q.get_nowait() == \"b\"\n    q.task_done()\n    q.task_done()\n    r2 = repr(q)\n    assert \"queue=\" not in r2\n    assert \"tasks=\" not in r2\n\n\ndef test_repr_with_waiting_getter():\n    \"\"\"True branch for _getters.\"\"\"\n\n    async def main():\n        q = Queue()\n        getter = asyncio.ensure_future(q.get())\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.05)\n        r = repr(q)\n        assert \"getters[1]\" in r\n        assert \"queue=\" not in r\n        # Wake the getter with an item so it finishes cleanly.\n        q.put_nowait(\"x\")\n        assert await getter == \"x\"\n        assert \"getters[\" not in repr(q)\n\n    _run(main())\n\n\ndef test_repr_with_waiting_putter():\n    \"\"\"True branch for _putters.\"\"\"\n\n    async def main():\n        q = Queue(maxsize=1)\n        q.put_nowait(\"a\")\n        putter = asyncio.ensure_future(q.put(\"b\"))\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.05)\n        r = repr(q)\n        assert \"putters[1]\" in r\n        assert \"queue=deque(['a'])\" in r\n        assert \"tasks=1\" in r\n        # Free a slot so the putter completes cleanly.\n        assert q.get_nowait() == \"a\"\n        q.task_done()\n        await putter\n        assert q.qsize() == 1\n        assert \"putters[\" not in repr(q)\n\n    _run(main())\n```\n\nWait, in `test_repr_with_waiting_getter`, after `q.put_nowait(\"x\")`, the getter future is resolved; `await getter` returns, then `get` returns `self.get_nowait()` = \"x\". Good. And `_getters` \u2014 the waiter was popped by `_wakeup_next` when put_nowait was called. So repr after shows no getters. Good.\n\nIn `test_repr_with_waiting_putter`: after `q.get_nowait()` \u2014 hmm wait, `get_nowait` calls `_wakeup_next(self._putters)`, which pops the putter waiter and resolves it. Then `await putter` \u2014 the put coroutine resumes, calls `put_nowait(\"b\")`, returns. Then q has [\"b\"], qsize 1. And `_putters` is empty. Good.\n\nBut careful: `q.get_nowait()` \u2014 does it decrement `_unfinished_tasks`? No! `task_done` does. `get_nowait` just pops. So after get_nowait, tasks=1 still. I call `q.task_done()` for the first item. Then `await putter` \u2192 put_nowait(\"b\") \u2192 tasks=2. Then repr would show \"tasks=2\" and \"queue=deque(['b'])\". My assertion `\"putters[\" not in repr(q)` holds. I don't assert on tasks there. Fine. But for cleanliness, maybe call task_done again? Not necessary; queue is local. But let me keep assertions meaningful.\n\nHmm, one issue: in `test_repr_with_waiting_putter`, between `q.get_nowait()` and `await putter`, the putter future was already resolved by `_wakeup_next` inside `get_nowait`. `await putter` \u2014 the task `putter` is `asyncio.ensure_future(q.put(\"b\"))`; awaiting the task waits for the coroutine to finish. The coroutine resumes on the next loop iteration. `await putter` yields control, loop resumes the put task, it completes. Good.\n\nEdge: `asyncio.sleep(0.05)` \u2014 is one iteration enough? `ensure_future` schedules the corout